    {
      "id": "aOfmvw8pHKS1-TiYLYScB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 521,
      "y": 168.5,
      "width": 847.9000244140625,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bG0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1118072907,
      "version": 343,
      "versionNonce": 1460844971,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779356006491,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Exercise 3.38: Suppose that Peter, Paul, and Mary share a joint bank account\nthat initially contains $100. Concurrently, Peter deposits $10, Paul withdraws\n$20, and Mary withdraws half the money in the account, by executing the\nfollowing commands:\n\nPeter: (set! balance (+ balance 10))\nPaul: (set! balance (- balance 20))\nMary: (set! balance (- balance (/ balance 2)))\n\na. List all the different possible values for balance after these three transactions\nhave been completed, assuming that the banking system forces the three processes\nto run sequentially in some order.\n\n1. Peter/Paul/Mary: 110 -> 90 -> 45\n2. Peter/Mary/Paul: 110 -> 55 -> 35\n3. Paul/Mary/Peter: 80 -> 40 -> 50\n4. Mary/Peter/Paul: 50 -> 60 -> 40\n\nb. What are some other values that could be produced if the system allows the\nprocesses to be interleaved?\nDraw timing diagrams like the one in Figure 3.29 to explain how these values can occur.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Exercise 3.38: Suppose that Peter, Paul, and Mary share a joint bank account\nthat initially contains $100. Concurrently, Peter deposits $10, Paul withdraws\n$20, and Mary withdraws half the money in the account, by executing the\nfollowing commands:\n\nPeter: (set! balance (+ balance 10))\nPaul: (set! balance (- balance 20))\nMary: (set! balance (- balance (/ balance 2)))\n\na. List all the different possible values for balance after these three transactions\nhave been completed, assuming that the banking system forces the three processes\nto run sequentially in some order.\n\n1. Peter/Paul/Mary: 110 -> 90 -> 45\n2. Peter/Mary/Paul: 110 -> 55 -> 35\n3. Paul/Mary/Peter: 80 -> 40 -> 50\n4. Mary/Peter/Paul: 50 -> 60 -> 40\n\nb. What are some other values that could be produced if the system allows the\nprocesses to be interleaved?\nDraw timing diagrams like the one in Figure 3.29 to explain how these values can occur.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
